const newPost1EN = { slug: 'start-online-store-saudi-arabia', category: 'guide',
  title: 'How to Start an Online Store in Saudi Arabia: A Step-by-Step Guide for 2026',
  excerpt: 'You want to sell online but have no idea where to start. Platform, payments, shipping, legal — here is exactly what to do in order, with real costs for each step.',
  date: '2026-06-01', author: 'BOSLA Team', readingTime: '12 min read',
  content: `<p>Ahmad had been selling perfumes through his Instagram account for two years. He had 8,000 followers, consistent engagement, and a growing customer base. But every day was the same struggle — customers would comment asking about prices and availability, and he would spend hours responding to DMs, sending bank account numbers, and manually tracking orders in a notebook. He knew he needed a proper online store, but every time he searched "how to start an online store in Saudi Arabia," he found conflicting advice. Three months later, he was still on Instagram, still losing sales to competitors with proper stores. If this sounds familiar, you are not alone. This guide walks you through exactly what to do in the right order.</p><h2>Step 1: Choose Your E-commerce Platform</h2><p>Your platform choice is the most important decision. It determines your monthly costs, which payment gateways you can use, how your store looks on mobile, and how easy it is to manage daily operations. In Saudi Arabia, you have three strong options. <strong>Salla</strong> is the most popular choice for Saudi startups. It costs 149 SAR per month, comes with Mada and STC Pay built in, and has a fully Arabic dashboard. You can launch a basic store in 2-3 days. <strong>Shopify</strong> costs $39 per month (about 150 SAR) plus transaction fees and requires third-party apps for Saudi payment gateways. It is best if you plan to sell internationally or need advanced customization. <strong>Zid</strong> costs 249 SAR per month and offers a middle ground — Arabic interface with more flexibility than Salla. For most new Saudi merchants, we recommend starting with Salla as the fastest and most affordable path to launch.</p><h2>Step 2: Register Your Business</h2><p>To accept payments and build customer trust, you need proper business registration. At minimum, you need a Commercial Registration (CR) from the Ministry of Commerce. You also need to register with ZATCA for VAT if your annual revenue exceeds 375,000 SAR. For specific product categories like food, cosmetics, or health items, additional permits from the Saudi Food and Drug Authority (SFDA) may be required. Many new store owners skip this step and later face issues with payment gateway approval and customer trust.</p><h2>Step 3: Set Up Payment Gateways</h2><p>Saudi customers expect to pay with Mada, STC Pay, Apple Pay, and increasingly with Buy Now Pay Later options like Tabby and Tamara. If your store only offers credit card payments, you will lose a significant portion of potential customers. Each payment gateway requires a separate application and approval process. Mada integration through providers like Tap, Moyasar, or HyperPay typically takes 1-2 weeks for approval. STC Pay has a similar timeline. Tabby and Tamara applications may take longer depending on your business documentation.</p><h2>Step 4: Set Up Shipping and Logistics</h2><p>In Saudi Arabia, Cash on Delivery still accounts for 30-50% of e-commerce orders. You need a shipping partner who can handle COD collections reliably. Major options include SMSA for comprehensive Saudi coverage, Aramex for both domestic and international shipping, and Naqel for reliable nationwide delivery. For local delivery within a single city, services like Mrsool and Luggzy work well for restaurants and perishable goods. Factor shipping costs into your pricing strategy — many Saudi stores offer free shipping above a certain order value to encourage larger purchases.</p><h2>Step 5: Add Products Optimized for Search</h2><p>This is where most new store owners make their biggest mistake. They upload products with generic descriptions copied from suppliers and wonder why nobody visits their store. Each product needs a unique title that includes relevant search keywords, a detailed description that answers customer questions, high-quality images from multiple angles, proper categorization within your store structure, and SEO metadata including meta title and description for Google. ${IL('/services/seo/ecommerce-seo?lang=en', 'E-commerce SEO')} is critical for getting found by customers searching on Google. Stores that invest in SEO from day one see significantly better results than those that try to add it later.</p><p>Starting an online store in Saudi Arabia is an exciting journey with enormous potential. The Saudi e-commerce market is projected to grow to nearly $50 billion by 2030. The businesses that succeed are the ones that take action — they pick a platform, set up properly, and start selling. They do not wait for perfection. If you need help with any part of this process, we offer free consultations to guide you through platform selection, store setup, SEO, and marketing strategy. ${IL('/services/web-development/online-stores?lang=en', 'Online store setup')} does not need to be complicated when you have the right partner.</p>`
};

